title: Godly Sorrow Worketh Repentance – Doctrine of Assurance
description: The sorrow of the world against the godly sorrow that works repentance: what repent means, the Corinthians getting right, and the fruits of godly sorrow. From the teachings on 2 Corinthians 7.
output: lessons/doctrine/topic-assurance-godly-sorrow.html

# Built from the "Godly sorrow worketh repentance" teachings in studies/data/topics/assurance.json, in the author's words.

=== Introduction
# from 17858
--- Godly Sorrow Worketh Repentance [title]
overline: Doctrine · Assurance
heading: Godly Sorrow Worketh Repentance
sub: The sorrow of the world, held by Cain, and the godly sorrow, expressed by David.
refs: 2 Corinthians 7:9-11; Hebrews 12:11
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the study. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

# from 5456
--- The general rule
big: 2 Corinthians 7:10
lead: Note in verse 10, Paul is giving us a general rule, that godly sorrow, that is sincerely being sorry at a wrong you've done, leads many to the repentance of belief that is salvation, while the sorrow of this world to death.

=== What repent means
# from 14979, 12092
--- A change of mind
lead: Repent means to change your mind, to accept new information as true and have a change of heart, a conversion.
p: What people most often repent of are the false beliefs they have about God. About His judgment. About their own righteousness or how they can escape punishment for their sins.
- “Oh I thought idols would save me.”
- “I thought the law would save me.”
- “I thought maybe hell wasn't real.”
- “I didn't know my works weren't enough.”

# from 14979, 14794
--- Repent ye
kicker: Matthew 3
big: Matthew 3:2
panel-warn: Not this | He is not saying “get your life together and stop sinning, the Kingdom of Heaven is here.” False teachers have perverted “repent” into penance.
panel: But this | Here, John the Baptist is saying “Reconsider what you know and believe me.” They were baptized in the water confessing their sins, because they believed him, and were saved.

=== Two kinds of sorrow
# from 17855, 17858
--- Cain and David
lit: Cain | Genesis 4:13 | There was no remorse for what he had done, just sorrow at the consequence.
fig: David | Psalm 38:3 | David's sorrow is godly, he cannot bear his own iniquity and hates his sin that brought him low, rather than resenting the punishment he'd received.
callout: The burden Cain couldn't bear was the punishment, but he sorrowed not unto repentance. He never changed his mind about what he had done.

# from 5456
--- The sorrow of the world
panel-warn: The sorrow of the world | Being sorry you were caught, sorry you're in trouble, sorry you're paying a price for it, sorry that God has chastised you, this is worldly sorrow, and in it is death, because it isn't genuine, it's selfish.
panel: Godly sorrow | Godly sorrow, truly understanding the harm done to others or to God by our transgressions, can lead to repentance towards salvation, a change of mind that cannot be repented of.
callout: Faith in Christ, that cannot be lost once gained. Once saved, always saved.

=== The Corinthians
# from 5456
--- Those who had already believed
scripture: 2 Corinthians 7:8-9
lead: Is there a biblical example of repentance from sin? Yes. But it's not regarding getting saved (not directly), but of those who had already believed getting right.
p: So Paul isn't telling the Corinthians that their turning from the sin he pointed out among them is how they were saved, for they were already, but makes this comment as a part of a larger point, regarding their acceptance of his rebuke.

# from 5457
--- Sorrow is not itself repentance
lead: This sincere sorrow for sin is not itself repentance, but rather, repentance is the work of that sorrow.
p: Amen, this is the case of many, brought to repent unto Christ for the forgiveness of sins, and relief from the godly sorrow and guilt weighing their conscience, lifted up by the Holy Spirit and the surety of the promise.
p: But again, that's not the only thing that worketh repentance. Our caring and meekness, our ready answer for our hope, our chaste conversation, our example, the light of Jesus Christ shining through us, bring many to repentance as well.
center: As many receive it in tears, many still receive the truth in joy.
refs: Jude 22; Jude 23

# from 5458
--- What carefulness it wrought
scripture: 2 Corinthians 7:11
p: This is a helpful list, both for those of us seeking to get right with God regarding sin in our life, and how we should act, and for judging rightly the sincerity, be it godly or earthly sorrow, of those who have wronged us and are seeking forgiveness and claim to be making amends.

# from 5458
--- The fruits of godly sorrow
- **Carefulness.** An understanding of what it is you've done, how it affects others, the ripple effects and implications for the harm you've caused, and the things that lead you to the sin. Not giving occasion to the flesh.
- **Clearing.** To make yourself as blameless as possible going forward, in careful consideration.
- **Indignation.** You should be indignant at what you've done. Outraged at yourself.
- **Fear.** The fear of the chastisement from God, and the natural repercussions of their actions.
- **Vehement desire, zeal, revenge.** Making an honest retribution for the harm caused, not begrudgingly, but with zeal.
callout: Now note again, these all are the fruits of godly sorrow, just as repentance is the coming to understanding, in this case through that sorrow. They are not the same thing.

=== Turning to the Father
# from 17858, 11437
--- The peaceable fruit
big: Hebrews 12:11
p: If that new man isn't fed, and they continue walking in the flesh, they are still saved by the knowledge of the truth, but that discernment is dulled and God may have to chasten the person to work godly sorrow in their heart.
refs: Hebrews 12:12-13

# from 12454, 16591
--- He came to himself
scripture: Luke 15:17-18
lead: When the son *came to himself*, is the moment of repentance.
p: It was that turning towards the Father that shows repentance. Not his sorrow and regret. Not his willingness to work, or wanting to make his father his lord.
callout: He doesn't want you to be sorry, or need you to be sorrowful, the Lord just wants you to admit it's wrong and stop it.

# from 12234
--- Go, and sin no more
kicker: John 8
big: John 8:11
p: Their real sin was their own adultery against God. The hypocrisy wasn't so much in their judgement of the woman, but in their unfaithfulness to the Lord.
p: And then Jesus, having shown them their own adulterous hearts, tells the woman to go and sin no more. Which spiritually, in that context of whoredoms against God, is a call to repentance towards Christ. (not a call to sinless perfection and works salvation)

=== Summary
# from 5456, 14979, 17858, 5457, 5458, 12454
--- In summary
point: A general rule | Godly sorrow leads many to the repentance of belief that is salvation, while the sorrow of this world to death.
point: Repent | To change your mind, to accept new information as true and have a change of heart.
point: Cain and David | Cain sorrowed at the consequence. David hated his sin that brought him low.
point: Sorrow is not repentance | Repentance is the work of that sorrow, and its fruits are not the same thing.
point: The Corinthians | Already saved, their godly sorrow led to their getting right with God.
point: Turn to the Father | It was that turning towards the Father that shows repentance.

